WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.972
Can an agent learn something reliable from past experiments?

00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:07.608
ExperimentWiki asks three questions.

00:00:07.954 --> 00:00:09.232
What knowledge can we keep?

00:00:09.577 --> 00:00:10.830
How do we repair the system?

00:00:11.162 --> 00:00:14.799
And when new experiments arrive, do our conclusions hold up?

00:00:15.895 --> 00:00:17.238
This is the system overview.

00:00:17.557 --> 00:00:22.299
Start on the left, where programs compare treatment and control groups and record

00:00:22.299 --> 00:00:23.153
the effects.

00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:25.693
Follow the arrows to the middle.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:30.615
Programs turn the evidence into wiki pages that an agent can read.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.765
On the right, new experiments test earlier predictions.

00:00:35.097 --> 00:00:39.839
Along the bottom, an audit traces mistakes back to the programs and rules that

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:40.551
produced them.

00:00:41.658 --> 00:00:44.457
Here is the same system in a simpler view.

00:00:44.789 --> 00:00:48.065
Within one experiment, compare treatment with control.

00:00:48.397 --> 00:00:53.709
Across experiments, look for the same change and check whether its effect repeats.

00:00:54.353 --> 00:00:57.887
The wiki records the direction and strength of the evidence.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:02.227
When it makes a prediction, the system registers that prediction before a new

00:01:02.227 --> 00:01:05.451
experiment can confirm or contradict it.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.422
Two different mechanisms keep the system up to date.

00:01:09.754 --> 00:01:14.641
On the left, new experiments test the knowledge while the programs stay fixed.

00:01:15.257 --> 00:01:20.489
In later experiments, the predicted direction held for 81 percent of high-confidence

00:01:20.489 --> 00:01:24.100
findings, 70 percent of evidence-grade findings,

00:01:24.277 --> 00:01:27.270
and 53 percent of preliminary findings.

00:01:27.576 --> 00:01:30.607
On the right, the system audits how an answer was produced.

00:01:31.674 --> 00:01:32.926
The distinction matters.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:38.375
A new result can change what the wiki says, even when the programs work exactly as

00:01:38.375 --> 00:01:38.970
intended.

00:01:39.603 --> 00:01:42.106
A faulty calculation is a different problem.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.912
Fix the program, then check that the repair works on cases it has not seen.

00:01:47.258 --> 00:01:49.670
The first mechanism updates knowledge.

00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:53.459
The second improves how that knowledge is produced.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.528
Why use a wiki here?

00:01:55.873 --> 00:02:00.167
A question about an experiment may depend on several earlier tests,

00:02:00.512 --> 00:02:02.435
including tests that disagree.

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:07.330
Retrieval brings passages to the model when someone asks a question.

00:02:07.675 --> 00:02:12.188
ExperimentWiki uses programs to compare the evidence ahead of time.

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:17.559
The agent can read the resulting page, and later experiments can change what that

00:02:17.559 --> 00:02:18.528
page says.
